      "text": "The archaeological site of Gunung Padang was first discovered by Dutch archaeologists in 1914. It is located in Karyamukti, West Java, Indonesia, and is considered one of the largest megalithic sites in Southeast Asia.",
      "date": "1914"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Research and Excavation",
      "text": "In the 1970s, further research and excavation were conducted by Indonesian archaeologists, revealing more about the site's structure and historical significance.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renewed Interest and Studies",
      "text": "In the early 2010s, Gunung Padang gained renewed interest from researchers and the public. Studies suggested that the site might be much older than previously thought, possibly dating back to 20,000 BCE.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Controversial Dating Claims",
      "text": "In 2012, a team led by geologist Danny Hilman Natawidjaja claimed that Gunung Padang could be the oldest pyramid-like structure in the world, based on geological surveys and radiocarbon dating.",
      "date": "2012"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Government Involvement",
      "text": "In 2014, the Indonesian government showed interest in the site, with President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono visiting and supporting further research to uncover its mysteries.",
      "date": "2014"
    }
  ]
}

