      "headline": "White Revolution",
      "text": "In 1963, Mohammad Reza Pahlavi launched the White Revolution, a series of reforms aimed at modernizing Iran and consolidating his power. These reforms included land redistribution, women's suffrage, and the promotion of industrial growth.",
      "date": "1963"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Exile of Ayatollah Khomeini",
      "text": "In 1964, Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini was exiled from Iran due to his opposition to the Shah's policies and reforms. He initially went to Turkey and later settled in Najaf, Iraq.",
      "date": "1964"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rising Opposition",
      "text": "Throughout the 1970s, opposition to the Shah's regime grew, fueled by economic struggles, political repression, and cultural discontent. Various groups, including religious clerics, leftists, and nationalists, began to organize against the Shah.",
      "date": "1970s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Black Friday",
      "text": "On September 8, 1978, a large protest in Tehran was violently suppressed by the Shah's military, resulting in the deaths of many protesters. This event, known as Black Friday, marked a turning point in the revolution, galvanizing further opposition.",
      "date": "1978-09-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Departure of the Shah",
      "text": "On January 16, 1979, Mohammad Reza Pahlavi left Iran for exile, initially traveling to Egypt. His departure marked the collapse of the Pahlavi dynasty.",
      "date": "1979-01-16"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return of Ayatollah Khomeini",
      "text": "Ayatollah Khomeini returned to Iran from exile on February 1, 1979, to a massive welcome from millions of Iranians. His return marked a significant moment in the revolution.",
      "date": "1979-02-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Islamic Republic",
      "text": "On April 1, 1979, following a national referendum, Iran was officially declared an Islamic Republic, with Ayatollah Khomeini as its Supreme Leader. This marked the end of the monarchical government and the beginning of theocratic rule.",
      "date": "1979-04-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hostage Crisis",
      "text": "On November 4, 1979, Iranian students seized the U.S. Embassy in Tehran, taking 52 American diplomats and citizens hostage. The crisis lasted 444 days and significantly impacted U.S.-Iran relations.",
      "date": "1979-11-04"
    }
  ]
}

