      "text": "The four-stroke cycle was first conceptualized by Alphonse Beau de Rochas in 1861. He outlined the principles of the four-stroke engine cycle in a pamphlet, which included the processes of intake, compression, power, and exhaust.",
      "date": "1861"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Operational Four-Stroke Engine",
      "text": "Nikolaus Otto built the first operational four-stroke engine in 1876. This engine, known as the Otto engine, successfully implemented the four-stroke cycle and became the prototype for modern internal combustion engines.",
      "date": "1876"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Patent of the Four-Stroke Engine",
      "text": "Nikolaus Otto patented the four-stroke engine in 1877, securing his design and the principles of the four-stroke cycle for commercial use.",
      "date": "1877"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Diesel Engine",
      "text": "Rudolf Diesel developed the diesel engine in 1892, which is a type of internal combustion engine that also operates on a four-stroke cycle but uses compression ignition instead of spark ignition.",
      "date": "1892"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Adoption in Automobiles",
      "text": "By the early 20th century, the four-stroke engine became the dominant engine type used in automobiles due to its efficiency and reliability.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Advancements in Four-Stroke Engine Technology",
      "text": "Throughout the 20th century, numerous advancements were made in four-stroke engine technology, including improvements in fuel injection, turbocharging, and emissions control.",
