    {
      "headline": "Introduction of 401(k) Plan",
      "text": "The 401(k) plan was introduced as part of the Revenue Act of 1978. It allowed employees to avoid being taxed on deferred compensation.",
      "date": "1978"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Implementation of 401(k) Provision",
      "text": "The 401(k) provision became effective on January 1, 1980, allowing employees to contribute a portion of their wages to individual accounts.",
      "date": "1980-01-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "IRS Clarification on 401(k) Plans",
      "text": "In 1981, the Internal Revenue Service (IRS) issued rules that clarified the use of 401(k) plans, leading to their widespread adoption.",
      "date": "1981"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Growth in Popularity",
      "text": "Throughout the 1980s, 401(k) plans grew in popularity as more employers began offering them as a retirement savings option.",
      "date": "1980s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Economic Growth and 401(k) Expansion",
      "text": "During the 1990s, economic growth and stock market gains led to significant increases in 401(k) plan assets.",
      "date": "1990s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Enactment of the Economic Growth and Tax Relief Reconciliation Act",
      "text": "In 2001, the Economic Growth and Tax Relief Reconciliation Act (EGTRRA) was enacted, which increased contribution limits and added catch-up contributions for those over 50.",
      "date": "2001"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Pension Protection Act",
      "text": "The Pension Protection Act of 2006 was signed into law, providing more protection for 401(k) plan participants and encouraging automatic enrollment.",
      "date": "2006"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Roth 401(k)",
      "text": "In 2006, the Roth 401(k) option was introduced, allowing after-tax contributions with tax-free withdrawals in retirement.",
      "date": "2006"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Impact of the Great Recession",
