      "headline": "Founding of Goguryeo",
      "text": "Goguryeo was founded in 37 BCE by Jumong, who became its first king. The kingdom was established in the region of modern-day Northeast China and the northern Korean Peninsula.",
      "date": "-0037"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of King Taejo",
      "text": "King Taejo reigned from 53 to 146 CE and expanded Goguryeo's territory significantly. He established a centralized government and strengthened the kingdom's military.",
      "date": "0053"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Goguryeo–Wei War",
      "text": "In 244 CE, Goguryeo was invaded by the Wei dynasty of China. The Goguryeo capital, Hwando, was destroyed, but the kingdom eventually recovered.",
      "date": "0244"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of King Gwanggaeto the Great",
      "text": "King Gwanggaeto the Great reigned from 391 to 413 CE. He expanded Goguryeo's territory to its greatest extent, including parts of Manchuria and the Korean Peninsula.",
      "date": "0391"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Goguryeo–Sui Wars",
      "text": "Between 598 and 614 CE, Goguryeo successfully repelled multiple invasions by the Sui dynasty of China, maintaining its independence and territorial integrity.",
      "date": "0598"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Goguryeo–Tang Wars",
      "text": "From 645 to 668 CE, Goguryeo engaged in a series of conflicts with the Tang dynasty of China. Despite initial successes, Goguryeo eventually fell to a combined Tang and Silla force.",
      "date": "0645"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fall of Goguryeo",
      "text": "Goguryeo was conquered in 668 CE by the allied forces of the Tang dynasty and the Korean kingdom of Silla, marking the end of the kingdom.",
      "date": "0668"
    }
  ]
}

