      "text": "The star and crescent symbol was developed in the Greek colony of Byzantium around 300 BC.",
      "date": "-0300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Royal Emblem of Mithridates VI",
      "text": "The star and crescent became more widely used as the royal emblem of Pontic king Mithridates VI Eupator after he incorporated Byzantium into his kingdom for a short period.",
      "date": "-0120" 	},
    {
      "headline": "Use in Sassanian Empire",
      "text": "During the 5th century, the star and crescent symbol was present in coins minted by the Persian Sassanian Empire.",
      "date": "0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Continuation in Sassanian Coinage",
      "text": "The symbol was represented in the coins minted across the Sassanian Empire throughout the Middle East for more than 400 years from the 3rd century until the fall of the Sassanians after the Muslim conquest of Persia in the 7th century.",
      "date": "0220"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption by Early Caliphate",
      "text": "After the Muslim conquest of Persia in the 7th century, the conquering Muslim rulers kept the star and crescent symbol in their coinage during the early years of the caliphate, as the coins were exact replicas of the Sassanian coins.",
      "date": "0650"
    }
  ]
}

