      "headline": "Proto-Isaiah",
      "text": "Proto-Isaiah, containing the words of the 8th-century BC prophet Isaiah ben Amoz, is believed to have been composed during the 8th century BCE. This section includes chapters 1–39 and focuses on themes of judgment and restoration for Judah and Jerusalem.",
      "date": "-0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Deutero-Isaiah",
      "text": "Deutero-Isaiah, also known as 'the Book of Consolation', is attributed to an anonymous author writing during the Babylonian Exile in the 6th century BCE. This section includes chapters 40–55 and offers messages of hope and redemption.",
      "date": "-0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trito-Isaiah",
      "text": "Trito-Isaiah is believed to have been composed after the return from the Babylonian Exile, possibly in the late 6th century BCE. This section includes chapters 56–66 and addresses the restoration of Jerusalem and the community.",
      "date": "-0539"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Johann Christoph Döderlein's Hypothesis",
      "text": "In 1775, Johann Christoph Döderlein suggested that the Book of Isaiah contained the works of two prophets separated by more than a century.",
      "date": "1775"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bernhard Duhm's Theory",
      "text": "In the late 19th century, Bernhard Duhm proposed that the Book of Isaiah comprises three separate collections of oracles: Proto-Isaiah, Deutero-Isaiah, and Trito-Isaiah. This view became the consensus through most of the 20th century.",
      "date": "1892"
    }
  ]
}

