    {
      "headline": "Anu's Role in Mesopotamian Religion",
      "text": "Anu, originally known as An, was the divine personification of the sky and regarded as the king of the gods in ancient Mesopotamian religion. He was considered the ancestor of many deities and a source of divine and human kingship.",
      "date": "-3000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Association with Eanna Temple",
      "text": "It is sometimes proposed that the Eanna temple located in Uruk originally belonged to Anu, rather than Inanna. However, while Anu is well attested as one of its divine inhabitants, there is no evidence that the main deity of the temple ever changed from Inanna, who was already associated with it in the earliest sources.",
      "date": "-3000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Passive Role in Worship",
      "text": "Despite his high status, Anu's role was largely passive, and he was not commonly worshipped. His presence was more symbolic, representing the authority and order of the divine hierarchy.",
      "date": "-2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Redefinition under Seleucid Rule",
      "text": "After the decline of the Eanna temple, a new theological system developed in Uruk under Seleucid rule, resulting in Anu being redefined as an active deity. This change led to Anu being actively worshipped by the inhabitants of the city in the final centuries of ancient Mesopotamia's history.",
      "date": "-300"
    }
  ]
}

