      "headline": "Announcement of the Partition",
      "text": "The first Partition of Bengal was announced on 20 July 1905 by Lord Curzon, the Viceroy of India. The partition aimed to separate the largely Muslim eastern areas from the largely Hindu western areas.",
      "date": "1905-07-20"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Implementation of the Partition",
      "text": "The partition was implemented on 16 October 1905, creating the new province of Eastern Bengal and Assam, with Dhaka as its capital.",
      "date": "1905-10-16"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of Eastern Bengal and Assam",
      "text": "The new province of Eastern Bengal and Assam was formed as a result of the partition, with Dhaka as its capital, and it included the eastern districts of Bengal and Assam.",
      "date": "1905-10-16"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Protests and Boycotts",
      "text": "The partition led to widespread protests and boycotts by the Indian National Congress and other nationalist groups, who saw it as an attempt to divide and rule.",
      "date": "1905"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Swadeshi Movement",
      "text": "The Swadeshi Movement emerged as a response to the partition, encouraging the boycott of British goods and the promotion of Indian-made products.",
      "date": "1905"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Annulment of the Partition",
      "text": "The partition was annulled on 12 December 1911, during the Delhi Durbar, due to the continuous protests and unrest it caused.",
      "date": "1911-12-12"
    }
  ]
}

