      "text": "Pembrolizumab was developed by the pharmaceutical company Merck & Co. as a humanized antibody targeting the PD-1 receptor, which plays a role in the immune system's ability to attack cancer cells.",
      "date": "2006"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Breakthrough Therapy Designation",
      "text": "In April 2013, pembrolizumab received the FDA's Breakthrough Therapy Designation for the treatment of advanced melanoma, highlighting its potential as a significant improvement over existing therapies.",
      "date": "2013-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Melanoma",
      "text": "Pembrolizumab was approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for the treatment of advanced melanoma on September 4, 2014.",
      "date": "2014-09-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer",
      "text": "On October 2, 2015, pembrolizumab was approved by the FDA for the treatment of metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) in patients whose tumors express PD-L1.",
      "date": "2015-10-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Head and Neck Cancer",
      "text": "Pembrolizumab received FDA approval for the treatment of recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma on August 5, 2016.",
      "date": "2016-08-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Hodgkin Lymphoma",
      "text": "On March 14, 2017, pembrolizumab was approved by the FDA for the treatment of adult and pediatric patients with refractory classical Hodgkin lymphoma.",
      "date": "2017-03-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Gastric Cancer",
      "text": "The FDA approved pembrolizumab for the treatment of recurrent locally advanced or metastatic gastric or gastroesophageal junction adenocarcinoma on September 22, 2017.",
      "date": "2017-09-22"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Cervical Cancer",
      "text": "Pembrolizumab was approved by the FDA for the treatment of recurrent or metastatic cervical cancer with disease progression on or after chemotherapy on June 12, 2018.",
      "date": "2018-06-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Triple-Negative Breast Cancer",
      "text": "On November 13, 2020, pembrolizumab was approved by the FDA for the treatment of patients with locally recurrent unresectable or metastatic triple-negative breast cancer whose tumors express PD-L1.",
