      "headline": "Foundation of the Society for Psychical Research",
      "text": "In 1882, the Society for Psychical Research was founded in London. It was the first organization to conduct scholarly research into human experiences that challenge contemporary scientific models.",
      "date": "1882"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the American Society for Psychical Research",
      "text": "The American Society for Psychical Research was established in 1885, following the model of the British Society for Psychical Research, to investigate paranormal phenomena in the United States.",
      "date": "1885"
    },
    {
      "headline": "J.B. Rhine's Experiments at Duke University",
      "text": "In the 1930s, J.B. Rhine conducted experiments at Duke University, which led to the establishment of the term 'parapsychology' and the use of statistical methods to study psychic phenomena.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Foundation of the Parapsychological Association",
      "text": "In 1957, the Parapsychological Association was founded to advance parapsychology as a science, and it was later accepted as an affiliate of the American Association for the Advancement of Science in 1969.",
      "date": "1957"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'The Conscious Universe'",
      "text": "In 1997, Dean Radin published 'The Conscious Universe', which presented an overview of parapsychological research and argued for the existence of psychic phenomena.",
      "date": "1997"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Criticism and Decline in Academic Interest",
      "text": "By the late 20th and early 21st centuries, parapsychology faced significant criticism from the scientific community, leading to a decline in academic interest and funding.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

