    {
      "headline": "Development of Pure Fission Weapons",
      "text": "Pure fission weapons, the simplest and least technically demanding nuclear weapons, were the first to be developed. These weapons were used by the United States on Japan in World War II in 1945.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Boosted Fission Weapons",
      "text": "Boosted fission weapons were developed to increase the yield beyond that of the implosion design. By using small quantities of fusion fuel, these weapons enhance the fission chain reaction and can more than double the weapon's fission energy yield.",
      "date": "1950s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Staged Thermonuclear Weapons",
      "text": "Staged thermonuclear weapons, also known as hydrogen bombs, were developed. These weapons use a two-stage process where the first stage is a boosted fission weapon. Its detonation causes X-rays to implode the second stage filled with fusion fuel, resulting in a thermonuclear burn with yields hundreds of times greater than fission weapons.",
      "date": "1950s"
    }
  ]
}

