      "headline": "Emergence of Sanatoriums",
      "text": "Sanatoriums began to emerge in the late 19th century as specialized hospitals for the treatment of specific diseases, particularly tuberculosis. They were often located in healthy climates, usually in the countryside, to promote healing.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak Popularity of Sanatoriums",
      "text": "The concept of sanatoriums reached its peak popularity in the early 20th century. These facilities were seen as a place for healing not only for tuberculosis but also for other ailments such as alcoholism, hysteria, and emotional exhaustion.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Role of Charitable Associations",
      "text": "Many sanatoriums were operated by charitable associations, such as the Order of St. John, and newly founded social welfare insurance companies, reflecting the social welfare movements of the time.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of Sanatoriums",
      "text": "With the development of antibiotics and improved medical treatments for diseases like tuberculosis, the need for sanatoriums began to decline in the mid-20th century.",
      "date": "1950"
    }
  ]
}

