      "text": "Following the partitions of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth in the late 18th century, Belarusian territories became part of the Russian Empire, leading to a decline in the use of the Belarusian language.",
      "date": "1795"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Belarusian National Revival",
      "text": "The late 19th and early 20th centuries saw a Belarusian national revival, with increased interest in Belarusian culture and language, leading to the publication of Belarusian newspapers and books.",
      "date": "1890"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Belarusian Language in Soviet Belarus",
      "text": "After the establishment of Soviet Belarus in 1919, the Belarusian language was initially promoted as part of Soviet nationality policy, but later faced Russification.",
      "date": "1919"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Belarusian Language in Independent Belarus",
      "text": "Following the dissolution of the Soviet Union, Belarus declared independence in 1991, and the Belarusian language was declared the sole official language of the country.",
      "date": "1991"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Official Language Status",
      "text": "In 1995, a referendum led to Russian being given equal status with Belarusian as an official language in Belarus.",
      "date": "1995"
    }
  ]
}

