      "text": "In 1916, Man Ray co-founded the Society of Independent Artists, which aimed to provide exhibition opportunities for avant-garde artists.",
      "date": "1916"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Move to Paris",
      "text": "In 1921, Man Ray moved to Paris, France, where he became an integral part of the Dada and Surrealist movements.",
      "date": "1921"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Creation of Rayographs",
      "text": "In the early 1920s, Man Ray began creating photograms, which he called 'rayographs,' a technique that involved placing objects directly on photosensitive paper and exposing them to light.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Film 'Le Retour à la Raison'",
      "text": "In 1923, Man Ray created his first film, 'Le Retour à la Raison,' which featured his rayographs.",
      "date": "1923"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Les Champs Délicieux'",
      "text": "In 1922, Man Ray published 'Les Champs Délicieux,' a portfolio of his rayographs.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Collaboration with Marcel Duchamp",
      "text": "Throughout the 1920s, Man Ray collaborated with Marcel Duchamp on various projects, including the creation of the film 'Anémic Cinéma' in 1926.",
      "date": "1926"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Exhibition at the International Surrealist Exhibition",
      "text": "In 1936, Man Ray exhibited his work at the International Surrealist Exhibition in London.",
      "date": "1936"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return to the United States",
      "text": "In 1940, due to the outbreak of World War II, Man Ray returned to the United States, settling in Los Angeles, California.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return to Paris",
      "text": "In 1951, Man Ray returned to Paris, where he continued to work and live for the rest of his life.",
      "date": "1951"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Autobiography",
      "text": "In 1963, Man Ray published his autobiography, 'Self Portrait.'",
      "date": "1963"
