      "headline": "First Appearance of Echinoderms",
      "text": "The first definitive echinoderms appeared near the start of the Cambrian period, approximately 541 million years ago. This marks the beginning of their evolutionary history.",
      "date": "-0541000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Diversification in the Ordovician",
      "text": "During the Ordovician period, around 485 to 443 million years ago, echinoderms underwent significant diversification, leading to the development of many of the major groups that exist today.",
      "date": "-0485000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Echinoderm Extinction Event",
      "text": "At the end of the Permian period, approximately 252 million years ago, a mass extinction event occurred, which significantly affected echinoderm populations along with many other marine species.",
      "date": "-0252000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recovery and Radiation in the Mesozoic",
      "text": "Following the Permian extinction, echinoderms recovered and radiated during the Mesozoic era, which spans from about 252 to 66 million years ago. This era saw the rise of modern echinoderm groups.",
      "date": "-0252000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Echinoderms",
      "text": "Today, echinoderms are represented by about 7,600 living species, making them the second-largest group of deuterostomes after the chordates. They inhabit marine environments from the intertidal zone to the abyssal depths.",
      "date": "0000"
    }
  ]
}

