      "headline": "Ingo Swann Suggests Term 'Remote Viewing'",
      "text": "In December 1971, Ingo Swann suggested the term 'remote viewing' during an experiment at the American Society for Psychical Research in New York City.",
      "date": "1971-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Research at Stanford Research Institute",
      "text": "Physicists Russell Targ and Harold Puthoff, parapsychology researchers at Stanford Research Institute (SRI), began researching remote viewing and are credited with coining the term to distinguish it from clairvoyance.",
      "date": "1972"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Remote Viewing Research",
      "text": "In 1974, Targ and Puthoff published their findings on remote viewing in the scientific journal 'Nature', bringing wider attention to the concept.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "CIA and Stargate Project",
      "text": "In the 1970s, the CIA began funding research into remote viewing, which eventually led to the establishment of the Stargate Project, a secret U.S. Army unit tasked with investigating the potential military applications of psychic phenomena.",
      "date": "1978"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Closure of Stargate Project",
      "text": "In 1995, the Stargate Project was declassified and subsequently closed after a CIA report concluded that remote viewing had not been useful in any intelligence operation.",
      "date": "1995"
    }
  ]
}

