    {
      "headline": "Origin of the Idiom",
      "text": "The idiom 'China's final warning' originated during the Cold War era, particularly in the 1950s and 1960s. It refers to the numerous warnings issued by China to the United States regarding violations of Chinese airspace by American aircraft. Despite these warnings, no significant actions were taken, leading to the phrase being used ironically to denote a warning that carries no real consequences.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Usage in Soviet Union",
      "text": "The phrase became popular in the Soviet Union as an ironic idiom. It was used to mock the frequent but ineffective warnings issued by China, suggesting that they were not taken seriously by the international community.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cultural Impact",
      "text": "Over time, 'China's final warning' became a part of Russian and Soviet vernacular, symbolizing any threat or warning that is repeatedly issued without any follow-through or consequence.",
      "date": "1970"
    }
  ]
}

