    {
      "headline": "Development of Dead Hand System",
      "text": "During the Cold War, the Soviet Union developed the Dead Hand system, also known as Perimeter, as an automatic or semi-automatic nuclear weapons control system. The exact date of its development is not publicly known, but it was constructed to ensure a retaliatory nuclear strike capability even if the Soviet command structure was incapacitated.",
      "date": "1980s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Functionality of Dead Hand",
      "text": "The Dead Hand system is designed to detect nuclear explosions through seismic, light, radioactivity, and pressure sensors. If a nuclear strike is detected, it can initiate the launch of Russian intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs) by sending a pre-entered highest-authority order from the General Staff of the Armed Forces to command posts and individual silos.",
      "date": "1980s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-Soviet Era",
      "text": "After the dissolution of the Soviet Union, the Dead Hand system remains in use in the post-Soviet Russian Federation. It is normally switched off and is supposed to be activated during times of crisis.",
      "date": "1991"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Status in 2009",
      "text": "As of 2009, the Dead Hand system was reported to remain fully functional and able to serve its purpose when needed. There are differing accounts on whether the system, once activated, will launch missiles fully automatically or if there is still a human approval process involved.",
      "date": "2009"
    }
  ]
}

