      "headline": "First Recorded Use of Hydropower",
      "text": "The earliest recorded use of hydropower was in ancient Greece, where water wheels were used to grind grain into flour. This technology dates back to around 200 BCE.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Water Turbines",
      "text": "In the mid-19th century, the development of water turbines began, which significantly improved the efficiency of converting water flow into mechanical energy. This development laid the groundwork for modern hydroelectric power.",
      "date": "1840"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Hydroelectric Power Plant",
      "text": "The world's first hydroelectric power plant began operation on September 30, 1882, in Appleton, Wisconsin, USA. It produced 12.5 kilowatts of power, enough to light two paper mills and a house.",
      "date": "1882-09-30"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Niagara Falls Hydroelectric Project",
      "text": "In 1895, the Niagara Falls hydroelectric project was completed. It was one of the first large-scale hydroelectric power plants and marked a significant milestone in the use of hydropower for electricity generation.",
      "date": "1895"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hoover Dam Completion",
      "text": "The Hoover Dam, one of the most famous hydroelectric power plants, was completed in 1936. Located on the Colorado River, it was the largest hydroelectric facility in the world at the time.",
      "date": "1936"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Three Gorges Dam Construction Begins",
      "text": "Construction of the Three Gorges Dam in China began in 1994. It is the world's largest power station in terms of installed capacity (22,500 MW).",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Three Gorges Dam Becomes Fully Operational",
      "text": "The Three Gorges Dam became fully operational in 2012, marking a significant achievement in hydroelectric power generation.",
      "date": "2012"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hydropower's Role in Global Electricity",
      "text": "By 2023, hydropower supplied 15% of the world's electricity, generating almost 4,210 TWh, more than all other renewable sources combined and also more than nuclear power.",
      "date": "2023"
    }
  ]
}

