      "text": "On March 24, 1989, the Exxon Valdez oil supertanker struck Bligh Reef in Prince William Sound, Alaska, at 12:04 a.m. The tanker, owned by Exxon Shipping Company and bound for Long Beach, California, spilled more than 10 million US gallons (240,000 barrels) of crude oil over the next few days.",
      "date": "1989-03-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Immediate Response and Cleanup Efforts",
      "text": "In the days following the spill, efforts to contain and clean up the oil began. However, the response was criticized for being slow and inadequate, leading to widespread environmental damage.",
      "date": "1989-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Legal Proceedings and Fines",
      "text": "In the aftermath of the spill, Exxon faced numerous lawsuits and was eventually ordered to pay billions in fines and settlements. In 1994, a jury awarded $5 billion in punitive damages, which was later reduced to $507.5 million by the U.S. Supreme Court in 2008.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Long-term Environmental Impact",
      "text": "The Exxon Valdez oil spill had long-lasting effects on the environment, wildlife, and local communities. Studies have shown that some areas and species have still not fully recovered decades after the spill.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Exxon Valdez Renamed and Retired",
      "text": "The Exxon Valdez was repaired and renamed several times before being retired. It was eventually converted into an ore carrier and scrapped in 2012.",
      "date": "2012"
    }
  ]
}

