      "headline": "MIDI Specification Development",
      "text": "In 1981, the development of the MIDI specification began when Dave Smith of Sequential Circuits proposed the idea of a standard interface for electronic musical instruments.",
      "date": "1981"
    },
    {
      "headline": "MIDI 1.0 Specification Released",
      "text": "The MIDI 1.0 specification was released in August 1983, establishing a standardized protocol for electronic musical instruments to communicate with each other.",
      "date": "1983-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Public Demonstration of MIDI",
      "text": "The first public demonstration of MIDI was held in January 1983 at the Winter NAMM Show, where a Sequential Circuits Prophet-600 and a Roland Jupiter-6 were successfully connected.",
      "date": "1983-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption by Major Manufacturers",
      "text": "By 1985, major musical instrument manufacturers such as Yamaha, Roland, Korg, and others had adopted the MIDI standard, leading to its widespread use in the music industry.",
      "date": "1985"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of General MIDI",
      "text": "In 1991, the General MIDI (GM) standard was introduced, providing a standardized set of sounds and a consistent way to play MIDI files across different devices.",
      "date": "1991"
    },
    {
      "headline": "MIDI 2.0 Announced",
      "text": "In January 2019, the MIDI Manufacturers Association announced the development of MIDI 2.0, an update to the original MIDI protocol, at the Winter NAMM Show.",
      "date": "2019-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "MIDI 2.0 Specification Released",
      "text": "The MIDI 2.0 specification was officially released in January 2020, introducing new features such as increased resolution, more expressive control, and bidirectional communication.",
      "date": "2020-01"
    }
  ]
}

