      "headline": "Introduction of the Musket",
      "text": "The musket, a muzzle-loaded long gun, appeared in the early 16th century as a heavier variant of the arquebus, capable of penetrating plate armour.",
      "date": "1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of Heavy Armour",
      "text": "By the mid-16th century, the use of heavy armour declined, leading to the gradual disappearance of the heavier type of musket.",
      "date": "1550"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Musket as a Generic Term",
      "text": "The term 'musket' continued to be used as a generic term for smoothbore long guns until the mid-19th century.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Rifled Muskets",
      "text": "In the 19th century, rifled muskets using the Minié ball became common, marking a significant development in firearm technology.",
      "date": "1850"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Breech-loading Firearms",
      "text": "The development of breech-loading firearms using self-contained cartridges and the first reliable repeating rifles produced by Winchester Repeating Arms Company in 1860 led to the demise of the musket.",
      "date": "1860"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Musket Era",
      "text": "By the time repeating rifles became common, they were known simply as 'rifles', ending the era of the musket.",
      "date": "1860"
    }
  ]
}

