      "headline": "Pederasty in Ancient Greece",
      "text": "In classical antiquity, the most widespread and socially significant form of same-sex sexual relations in ancient Greece amongst elite circles was between adult men and pubescent or adolescent boys, known as pederasty. This practice was prevalent in various city-states, though the acceptance and regulation of such relationships varied.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Literary Exploration of Homosexuality",
      "text": "Writers such as Herodotus, Plato, Xenophon, and Athenaeus explored aspects of homosexuality in Greek society. These works provide insight into the cultural and social dynamics of same-sex relationships in ancient Greece.",
      "date": "-0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Plato's Symposium",
      "text": "Plato's 'Symposium', written around 385-370 BCE, is one of the most significant philosophical texts discussing love, including same-sex love, in ancient Greece. It presents various perspectives on love, including the idea of a higher form of love that transcends physical attraction.",
      "date": "-0385"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sappho and Female Homosexuality",
      "text": "Sappho, a poet from the island of Lesbos, is one of the earliest known figures to express female same-sex desire in her poetry. Her work dates back to around the 6th century BCE, providing evidence of the existence and expression of female homosexuality in ancient Greece.",
      "date": "-0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kenneth Dover's Analysis",
      "text": "In the 20th century, scholar Kenneth Dover analyzed ancient Greek homosexuality, suggesting that while sexual relationships between adult men did exist, at least one member of these relationships often flouted social conventions by assuming a passive sexual role. This analysis has been questioned by recent scholars.",
      "date": "1978"
    }
  ]
}

