      "headline": "Origins of Kathakali",
      "text": "Kathakali is believed to have originated in the late 16th century in the southwestern region of Kerala, India. It evolved from older forms of dance-drama, such as Koodiyattam and Krishnanattam.",
      "date": "1600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Kathakali",
      "text": "During the 17th century, Kathakali developed into a distinct form of dance-drama with its own unique style, incorporating elements of ancient martial arts, classical dance, and storytelling.",
      "date": "1700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence of King Kottarakkara Thampuran",
      "text": "King Kottarakkara Thampuran (c. 1555–1605) is credited with significant contributions to the development of Kathakali by adapting Krishnanattam and creating new stories, known as Attakatha, which are still performed today.",
      "date": "1600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Navarasas",
      "text": "Kathakali performances are renowned for their use of Navarasas, the nine emotions, which are integral to the art form and are based on Bharata Muni's Natya Shastra.",
      "date": "1600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Kathakali Schools",
      "text": "In the 18th and 19th centuries, several Kathakali schools, known as 'Kalasams', were established, each with its own distinct style and repertoire.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival and Global Recognition",
      "text": "In the 20th century, Kathakali gained international recognition as a classical art form, with efforts to preserve and promote it both in India and abroad.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

