      "text": "In 1896, just a year after the discovery of X-rays, they were first used to treat cancer, marking the beginning of radiation therapy.",
      "date": "1896"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of Radium",
      "text": "Marie and Pierre Curie discovered radium in 1898. Radium was later used in brachytherapy, a form of radiation therapy.",
      "date": "1898"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Brachytherapy",
      "text": "In the early 1900s, brachytherapy was developed, using radium to treat cancer by placing radioactive material directly into or near the tumor.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Cobalt-60",
      "text": "In the 1950s, Cobalt-60 machines were introduced, providing a more effective and safer means of delivering radiation therapy.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Linear Accelerators",
      "text": "In the 1950s and 1960s, linear accelerators were developed, allowing for more precise targeting of tumors with high-energy X-rays.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT)",
      "text": "In the 1990s, IMRT was introduced, allowing for more precise delivery of radiation doses to tumors while minimizing exposure to surrounding healthy tissue.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Image-Guided Radiation Therapy (IGRT)",
      "text": "In the early 2000s, IGRT was developed, using imaging techniques to improve the accuracy and precision of radiation therapy.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Proton Therapy",
      "text": "Proton therapy, a type of radiation therapy that uses protons rather than X-rays, began to be used more widely in the 2000s for its ability to deliver precise doses with less damage to surrounding tissue.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

