      "headline": "Establishment of the French Second Republic",
      "text": "The French Second Republic was established following the February Revolution of 1848, which led to the abdication of King Louis-Philippe and the end of the July Monarchy. The provisional government was formed on February 24, 1848.",
      "date": "1848-02-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption of the Constitution",
      "text": "The Constitution of the French Second Republic was adopted on November 4, 1848. It established a presidential system with a unicameral legislature.",
      "date": "1848-11-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Election of Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte",
      "text": "Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte was elected as the first President of the French Second Republic on December 10, 1848, winning a landslide victory.",
      "date": "1848-12-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "June Days Uprising",
      "text": "The June Days Uprising was a workers' revolt in Paris that took place from June 23 to June 26, 1848, in response to the closure of National Workshops, resulting in a violent confrontation with the government.",
      "date": "1848-06-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Coup d'état by Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte",
      "text": "On December 2, 1851, Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte staged a coup d'état, dissolving the National Assembly and establishing himself as the ruler of France, leading to the end of the Second Republic.",
      "date": "1851-12-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Proclamation of the Second French Empire",
      "text": "Following the successful coup, Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte proclaimed himself Emperor Napoleon III on December 2, 1852, marking the official end of the French Second Republic and the beginning of the Second French Empire.",
      "date": "1852-12-02"
    }
  ]
}

