      "headline": "Discovery of Snell's Law",
      "text": "Snell's Law was discovered by Willebrord Snellius in 1621. It describes the relationship between the angles of incidence and refraction when light passes through a boundary between two different isotropic media.",
      "date": "1621"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Snell's Law",
      "text": "Although discovered in 1621, Snell's Law was not published during Snellius's lifetime. It was first published posthumously by René Descartes in his book 'La Dioptrique' in 1637.",
      "date": "1637"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Application in Ray Tracing",
      "text": "Snell's Law is used in ray tracing to compute the angles of incidence or refraction, which is crucial for understanding how light behaves when passing through different media.",
      "date": "1637"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use in Experimental Optics",
      "text": "In experimental optics, Snell's Law is used to find the refractive index of a material, which is essential for designing lenses and optical instruments.",
      "date": "1637"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Application in Metamaterials",
      "text": "Snell's Law is also satisfied in metamaterials, which allow light to be bent 'backward' at a negative angle of refraction with a negative refractive index. This application was developed in the late 20th and early 21st centuries.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

