      "headline": "Discovery of Polonium",
      "text": "Polonium was discovered in 1898 by Marie Curie and Pierre Curie. They isolated the element while investigating radioactivity in uranium ores.",
      "date": "1898"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naming of Polonium",
      "text": "Marie Curie named the element 'polonium' after her homeland, Poland, as a gesture of patriotism and to bring attention to the political plight of Poland at the time.",
      "date": "1898"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Isolation of Polonium",
      "text": "Polonium was isolated from uranium ore by Marie and Pierre Curie, marking one of the first instances of isolating a radioactive element.",
      "date": "1898"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Production of Polonium",
      "text": "Today, polonium is produced in milligram quantities by the neutron irradiation of bismuth, a process developed in the mid-20th century.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use in Nuclear Weapons",
      "text": "Polonium was used in the initiators of early nuclear weapons, such as the Fat Man bomb dropped on Nagasaki in 1945.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use in Antistatic Devices",
      "text": "Polonium is used in antistatic devices, which eliminate static electricity in machinery and equipment.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
