      "headline": "Introduction of the Kármán Line Concept",
      "text": "The Kármán line was introduced as a concept to define the boundary between Earth's atmosphere and outer space. It is named after Theodore von Kármán, a Hungarian-American engineer and physicist who was the first to calculate that around this altitude, the atmosphere becomes too thin to support aeronautical flight.",
      "date": "1957"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FAI Adoption of the Kármán Line",
      "text": "The Fédération Aéronautique Internationale (FAI), the international record-keeping body for aeronautics and astronautics, adopted the Kármán line at an altitude of 100 kilometers (62 miles) above mean sea level as the boundary of space.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Debate on the Definition of Space",
      "text": "Over the years, the definition of the Kármán line has been debated, with some organizations and countries using different altitudes to define the boundary of space. Despite this, the 100-kilometer definition remains widely recognized.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

