      "headline": "Invention of Bump Stock",
      "text": "Bump stocks were invented in the early 2000s as a device to assist in bump firing, allowing semi-automatic firearms to mimic the firing speed of automatic firearms.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "ATF Approval",
      "text": "In 2010, the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives (ATF) approved the sale of bump stocks, determining that they did not convert a firearm into a machine gun.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Las Vegas Shooting",
      "text": "On October 1, 2017, a mass shooting occurred in Las Vegas, Nevada, where the shooter used rifles equipped with bump stocks, leading to 58 deaths and hundreds of injuries.",
      "date": "2017-10-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Public and Political Response",
      "text": "Following the Las Vegas shooting, there was significant public and political pressure to regulate or ban bump stocks.",
      "date": "2017-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "ATF Proposed Rule",
      "text": "In March 2018, the Department of Justice announced a proposed rule to classify bump stocks as machine guns, effectively banning them.",
      "date": "2018-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Final Rule Issued",
      "text": "On December 18, 2018, the Department of Justice issued a final rule classifying bump stocks as machine guns under federal law, with the rule taking effect in March 2019.",
      "date": "2018-12-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bump Stock Ban Takes Effect",
      "text": "On March 26, 2019, the federal ban on bump stocks took effect, requiring owners to destroy or surrender their devices.",
      "date": "2019-03-26"
    }
  ]
}

