      "headline": "Issuance of the Zimmermann Telegram",
      "text": "The Zimmermann Telegram was issued by the German Foreign Office on January 17, 1917. It proposed a military alliance between Germany and Mexico in the event that the United States entered World War I against Germany.",
      "date": "1917-01-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Interception by British Intelligence",
      "text": "The British intelligence intercepted the Zimmermann Telegram. The British cryptographic office, known as Room 40, decoded the message.",
      "date": "1917-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Disclosure to the United States",
      "text": "The British government presented the decoded Zimmermann Telegram to the United States on February 24, 1917. This disclosure was intended to sway American public opinion against Germany.",
      "date": "1917-02-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication in American Newspapers",
      "text": "The Zimmermann Telegram was published in American newspapers on March 1, 1917, causing a public outcry and increasing support for the United States to enter World War I.",
      "date": "1917-03-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Germany Confirms the Telegram's Authenticity",
      "text": "On March 3, 1917, German Foreign Secretary Arthur Zimmermann publicly admitted that the telegram was genuine, further inflaming American public opinion.",
      "date": "1917-03-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Impact on U.S. Entry into World War I",
      "text": "The revelation of the Zimmermann Telegram was a significant factor in the United States' decision to enter World War I, which it did on April 6, 1917.",
      "date": "1917-04-06"
    }
  ]
}

