    {
      "headline": "Introduction of CE Marking",
      "text": "The CE marking was introduced in 1985 as part of the European Union's New Approach to technical harmonization and standards. It was designed to facilitate trade within the European Economic Area by ensuring that products meet EU safety, health, and environmental protection requirements.",
      "date": "1985"
    },
    {
      "headline": "CE Marking Becomes Mandatory",
      "text": "In 1993, the CE marking became mandatory for certain product categories sold within the European Economic Area. This requirement was part of the implementation of the Single European Market.",
      "date": "1993"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion of CE Marking Requirements",
      "text": "Over the years, the scope of products requiring CE marking has expanded to include a wide range of goods, such as electronics, machinery, medical devices, and construction products. This expansion ensures that a broad spectrum of products meets EU standards.",
      "date": "1990s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "CE Marking and the European Economic Area",
      "text": "The CE marking is required for products sold in the European Economic Area (EEA), which includes the EU member states and several other countries. This requirement ensures that products can move freely within the EEA while adhering to consistent safety and environmental standards.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "CE Marking and Global Trade",
      "text": "Although the CE marking is primarily for the European Economic Area, many products sold globally also carry the mark to indicate compliance with EU standards. This practice helps manufacturers streamline their production processes and expand their market reach.",
      "date": "2000s"
    }
  ]
}

