{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Emergence of Hoplites",
      "text": "Hoplites began to emerge as a distinct class of citizen-soldiers in Ancient Greek city-states around the 7th century BC. This period marked the transition from aristocratic cavalry to infantry-based warfare, facilitated by the availability of iron weapons.",
      "date": "-0700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Phalanx Formation",
      "text": "The phalanx formation, a tactical formation that allowed hoplites to fight as a unit, became prominent. This formation required discipline and coordination, emphasizing the collective strength of the hoplites over individual prowess.",
      "date": "-0700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hoplites in the Stories of Homer",
      "text": "Hoplites are mentioned in the epic tales of Homer, suggesting their presence in Greek culture and warfare. However, their organized use in warfare likely began in earnest in the 7th century BC.",
      "date": "-0700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Iron Age and Accessibility of Weapons",
      "text": "During the Iron Age, weapons became more affordable, allowing ordinary citizens to arm themselves. This democratization of military equipment contributed to the rise of the hoplite class.",
      "date": "-0700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Role of Hoplites in Greek City-States",
      "text": "Hoplites were primarily free citizens, such as propertied farmers and artisans, who could afford their own armor and weapons. They played a crucial role in the defense and military campaigns of Greek city-states.",
      "date": "-0700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Professional Hoplite Units",
      "text": "Some Greek city-states, including Athens, Sparta, Argos, Thebes, and Syracuse, maintained small elite professional hoplite units known as the epilektoi or logades. These units were selected from the regular citizen infantry for their skill and bravery.",
      "date": "-0700"
    }
  ]
}

