      "headline": "First Release of eSIM",
      "text": "In March 2016, eSIM was first released as a global specification by the GSMA. This technology enables remote SIM provisioning, allowing end-users to change mobile network operators without the need to physically swap a SIM card from the device.",
      "date": "2016-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption in Flagship Devices",
      "text": "Since 2018, most flagship devices that are not SIM locked have supported eSIM technology. This marked a significant shift in the mobile telephony industry, as eSIMs began to replace traditional SIM cards in many high-end devices.",
      "date": "2018"
    },
    {
      "headline": "134 Mobile Phone Models Support eSIM",
      "text": "By October 2023, there were 134 models of mobile phones that supported eSIMs, highlighting the widespread adoption of this technology in the mobile phone industry.",
      "date": "2023-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "eSIM in Various Applications",
      "text": "eSIM technology has expanded beyond mobile phones to include tablet computers, smartwatches, and Internet of Things applications such as connected cars, artificial intelligence translators, MiFi devices, smart earphones, smart metering, GPS tracking units, database transaction units, bicycle-sharing systems, advertising players, and closed-circuit television cameras.",
      "date": "2023"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Future Projections for eSIM",
      "text": "A report stated that by 2025, 98% of mobile network operators were expected to offer eSIMs, indicating a near-universal adoption of this technology in the telecommunications industry.",
      "date": "2025"
    }
  ]
}

