    {
      "headline": "Founding of IUCN",
      "text": "The International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) was founded on October 5, 1948, in Fontainebleau, France. It was established as the first global environmental union, bringing together governments and civil society organizations.",
      "date": "1948-10-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First General Assembly",
      "text": "The first General Assembly of the IUCN was held in 1948, shortly after its founding, to establish its initial framework and objectives.",
      "date": "1948"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Red List of Threatened Species",
      "text": "In 1964, IUCN established the Red List of Threatened Species, a comprehensive inventory of the global conservation status of biological species.",
      "date": "1964"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World Conservation Strategy",
      "text": "In 1980, IUCN, along with the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) and the World Wildlife Fund (WWF), published the World Conservation Strategy, which outlined the principles of sustainable development.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Species Survival Commission",
      "text": "The IUCN Species Survival Commission (SSC) was established to provide scientific advice on species conservation in 1949.",
      "date": "1949"
    },
    {
