      "headline": "Roman Occupation",
      "text": "During the Roman period, the area now known as County Durham was part of the Roman Empire. The Romans built several forts in the region, including one at Binchester.",
      "date": "-0043"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Foundation of Durham Cathedral",
      "text": "Durham Cathedral was founded in 1093. It is one of the finest examples of Norman architecture and has been designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
      "date": "1093"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Neville's Cross",
      "text": "On October 17, 1346, the Battle of Neville's Cross took place near Durham. The English army defeated the Scots, capturing King David II of Scotland.",
      "date": "1346-10-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Industrial Revolution",
      "text": "During the Industrial Revolution, County Durham became a major center for coal mining and railways, significantly contributing to the region's economic growth.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of County Durham",
      "text": "The modern administrative county of Durham was formed in 1889, following the Local Government Act 1888.",
      "date": "1889"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Closure of Coal Mines",
      "text": "The coal mines in County Durham began to close in the mid-20th century, with the last deep mine closing in 1994, marking the end of an era for the region.",
      "date": "1994"
    }
  ]
}

