{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "First Recorded Lottery",
      "text": "The first recorded signs of a lottery are keno slips from the Chinese Han Dynasty between 205 and 187 BCE. These lotteries are believed to have helped finance major government projects like the Great Wall of China.",
      "date": "-0205"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Roman Empire Lotteries",
      "text": "Lotteries were also used in the Roman Empire, mainly as a form of amusement at dinner parties. Each guest would receive a ticket, and prizes would often consist of fancy items such as dinnerware.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First European Lottery",
      "text": "The first recorded European lottery was held in the 15th century in the Low Countries. Towns held public lotteries to raise money for town fortifications and to help the poor.",
      "date": "1440"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Queen Elizabeth I's Lottery",
      "text": "In 1569, England's first lottery was held, with Queen Elizabeth I establishing it to raise funds for the reparation of harbours and other public works.",
      "date": "1569"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First American Lottery",
      "text": "The first American lottery was held in 1612, when King James I of England created a lottery to provide funds to the Jamestown settlement in Virginia.",
      "date": "1612"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lotteries in Colonial America",
      "text": "Lotteries were used in colonial America to finance roads, libraries, churches, colleges, canals, and bridges. The colonies held over 200 lotteries between 1744 and 1776.",
      "date": "1744"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lottery Ban in the United States",
      "text": "By the beginning of the 20th century, most forms of gambling, including lotteries, were illegal in the United States.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Re-emergence of Lotteries",
      "text": "In the 1960s, lotteries began to re-appear throughout the world as a means for governments to raise revenue without raising taxes.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
