{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Ancient Origins",
      "text": "The concept of hysteria dates back to ancient times, with the term deriving from the Greek word 'hystera' meaning uterus. Ancient Greek physicians like Hippocrates believed that hysteria was caused by a wandering uterus.",
      "date": "-0460"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Middle Ages Perception",
      "text": "During the Middle Ages, female hysteria was often associated with witchcraft and demonic possession. Women exhibiting symptoms were sometimes subjected to exorcisms or witch trials.",
      "date": "1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "17th Century Medicalization",
      "text": "In the 17th century, hysteria began to be viewed more as a medical condition rather than a supernatural one. Physicians like Thomas Sydenham described it as a disease of the nervous system.",
      "date": "1600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "19th Century Popularity",
      "text": "By the 19th century, hysteria was one of the most diagnosed diseases in women. Treatments included rest cures, hydrotherapy, and even surgical procedures like hysterectomy.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Freud's Contribution",
      "text": "Sigmund Freud, in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, contributed to the understanding of hysteria by linking it to psychological causes rather than physical ones, suggesting it was a result of repressed emotions.",
      "date": "1895"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of Diagnosis",
      "text": "In the early 20th century, the diagnosis of hysteria began to decline as medical understanding of mental health improved. The condition was gradually replaced by more specific diagnoses such as anxiety disorders and depression.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Removal from DSM",
      "text": "In 1980, the American Psychiatric Association removed hysteria from the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM), marking the end of its recognition as a legitimate medical diagnosis.",
      "date": "1980"
    }
  ]
}

{
