      "headline": "Emergence of Romanesque Architecture",
      "text": "Romanesque architecture began to emerge in the late 10th century, characterized by its semicircular arches, massive quality, thick walls, sturdy piers, groin vaults, large towers, and decorative arcading. It was the first pan-European architectural style since Imperial Roman architecture.",
      "date": "0980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak of Romanesque Architecture",
      "text": "The Romanesque style reached its peak during the 11th and 12th centuries, with notable examples found across Europe, including the Abbey Church of Sainte-Foy in France and the Speyer Cathedral in Germany.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transition to Gothic Architecture",
      "text": "By the late 12th century, Romanesque architecture began to evolve into the Gothic style, marked by the introduction of pointed arches, ribbed vaults, and flying buttresses. This transition is exemplified by structures such as the Basilica of Saint-Denis in France.",
      "date": "1190"
    }
  ]
}

