      "headline": "Composition of the Upanishads",
      "text": "The Upanishads, which form the basis of Vedanta philosophy, were composed over several centuries, starting around 800 BCE. They are considered the end part of the Vedas and focus on spiritual knowledge and philosophy.",
      "date": "-0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Composition of the Brahma Sutras",
      "text": "The Brahma Sutras, also known as the Vedanta Sutras, were composed by Badarayana around 400-200 BCE. They systematize and summarize the philosophical teachings of the Upanishads.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Composition of the Bhagavad Gita",
      "text": "The Bhagavad Gita, a 700-verse Hindu scripture that is part of the Indian epic Mahabharata, was composed around the 2nd century BCE. It is a dialogue between Prince Arjuna and Krishna, who serves as his charioteer.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Advaita Vedanta",
      "text": "Advaita Vedanta, a non-dualistic school of Vedanta philosophy, was developed by Adi Shankaracharya in the 8th century CE. It emphasizes the idea that the individual soul (Atman) and the ultimate reality (Brahman) are one.",
      "date": "0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Vishishtadvaita Vedanta",
      "text": "Vishishtadvaita Vedanta, a qualified non-dualistic school of Vedanta, was developed by Ramanuja in the 11th century CE. It emphasizes the idea that the soul and God are distinct but inseparable.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Dvaita Vedanta",
      "text": "Dvaita Vedanta, a dualistic school of Vedanta, was developed by Madhvacharya in the 13th century CE. It emphasizes the distinction between the individual soul and the ultimate reality.",
      "date": "1200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of other Vedanta schools",
      "text": "Several other schools of Vedanta, such as Bhedabheda, Achintya Bheda Abheda, and Shuddhadvaita, developed over the centuries, each offering unique interpretations of the Vedantic texts.",
      "date": "1300"
    }
  ]
}

