      "text": "On May 10, 1933, the German Student Union organized the first major book burning event in Berlin. Thousands of books deemed 'un-German' were burned in a public ceremony, including works by Jewish, communist, socialist, and other authors opposed to Nazi ideology.",
      "date": "1933-05-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nationwide Book Burnings",
      "text": "Following the initial event in Berlin, similar book burning ceremonies took place across Germany. These events were part of a broader campaign to cleanse German culture of foreign and subversive influences.",
      "date": "1933-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Targeting of Specific Authors",
      "text": "Throughout 1933, books by authors such as Karl Marx, Karl Kautsky, Albert Einstein, and Helen Keller were specifically targeted for burning due to their ideologies and backgrounds.",
      "date": "1933"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cultural Genocide in Occupied Territories",
      "text": "During the late 1930s and into the 1940s, the Nazis extended their book burning campaign to occupied territories, including Poland, as part of a broader effort to suppress and destroy cultural identities.",
      "date": "1939"
    }
  ]
}

