      "headline": "Announcement of Disengagement Plan",
      "text": "On December 18, 2003, Israeli Prime Minister Ariel Sharon announced his intention to unilaterally disengage from the Gaza Strip and parts of the northern West Bank.",
      "date": "2003-12-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Approval of Disengagement Plan",
      "text": "On June 6, 2004, the Israeli government approved the disengagement plan, which included the evacuation of all Israeli settlements in the Gaza Strip and four settlements in the northern West Bank.",
      "date": "2004-06-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Knesset Approval",
      "text": "On October 26, 2004, the Knesset approved the disengagement plan with a vote of 67 in favor, 45 against, and 7 abstentions.",
      "date": "2004-10-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Evacuation of Settlers Begins",
      "text": "On August 15, 2005, the evacuation of Israeli settlers from the Gaza Strip began. This marked the start of the physical implementation of the disengagement plan.",
      "date": "2005-08-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of Gaza Evacuation",
      "text": "By September 12, 2005, the evacuation of all Israeli settlers and military personnel from the Gaza Strip was completed.",
      "date": "2005-09-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of West Bank Evacuation",
      "text": "On September 22, 2005, the evacuation of the four settlements in the northern West Bank was completed.",
      "date": "2005-09-22"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formal End of Military Presence",
      "text": "On September 12, 2005, Israel formally ended its military presence in the Gaza Strip, although it continued to control the airspace and maritime access.",
      "date": "2005-09-12"
    }
  ]
}

