      "headline": "Introduction of African Honey Bees to Brazil",
      "text": "In 1956, African honey bees (A. m. scutellata) were brought to Brazil by biologist Warwick E. Kerr. The goal was to breed bees that would adapt better to tropical climates and increase honey production.",
      "date": "1956"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Accidental Release of Africanized Bees",
      "text": "In 1957, 26 swarms of Africanized bees escaped quarantine in Brazil. This accidental release led to the spread of Africanized bees throughout South and Central America.",
      "date": "1957"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spread to Central America",
      "text": "By the 1980s, Africanized bees had spread northward through Central America, reaching countries such as Panama and Costa Rica.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arrival in the United States",
      "text": "In October 1990, Africanized bees were first detected in the United States in the state of Texas. Their presence marked the beginning of their spread across the southern U.S.",
      "date": "1990-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spread Across the Southern United States",
      "text": "Throughout the 1990s and 2000s, Africanized bees continued to spread across the southern United States, reaching states such as Arizona, New Mexico, and California.",
      "date": "1990s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Current Distribution",
      "text": "As of the 2020s, Africanized bees are found in many parts of the Americas, from Argentina to the southern United States, adapting to various environments and impacting local ecosystems.",
      "date": "2020s"
    }
  ]
}

