      "headline": "Patent of Dvorak Keyboard Layout",
      "text": "The Dvorak Simplified Keyboard was patented by Dr. August Dvorak and his brother-in-law, Dr. William Dealey, in 1936. It was designed as a faster and more ergonomic alternative to the QWERTY layout.",
      "date": "1936"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Initial Adoption and Testing",
      "text": "In the years following its patent, the Dvorak keyboard layout underwent various tests and was adopted by some typists who reported increased speed and comfort.",
      "date": "1936-1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War II Navy Study",
      "text": "During World War II, the U.S. Navy conducted a study on the Dvorak layout, which showed that typists could increase their speed and accuracy after switching from QWERTY to Dvorak.",
      "date": "1940s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Typewriting Behavior'",
      "text": "In 1936, Dr. August Dvorak published 'Typewriting Behavior', a book that detailed the research and rationale behind the Dvorak Simplified Keyboard.",
      "date": "1936"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Inclusion in ANSI Standard",
      "text": "The Dvorak Simplified Keyboard was included in the American National Standards Institute (ANSI) standard for keyboards, recognizing it as an alternative layout.",
      "date": "1982"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dvorak Layout in Computer Systems",
      "text": "With the advent of personal computers, the Dvorak layout became available as an option in many operating systems, allowing users to switch from QWERTY.",
      "date": "1980s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Continued Advocacy and Use",
      "text": "Despite not becoming the dominant keyboard layout, the Dvorak layout continues to have a dedicated following and is advocated for its ergonomic benefits.",
      "date": "1990s-Present"
    }
  ]
}

