      "headline": "Start of the Khilafat Movement",
      "text": "The Khilafat Movement began in 1919 as a response to the dismemberment of the Ottoman Empire by the Allied forces after World War I. Indian Muslims launched this political campaign to protect the Ottoman Caliphate.",
      "date": "1919"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the All-India Khilafat Committee",
      "text": "In 1919, the All-India Khilafat Committee was formed to organize protests and campaigns against the British government's policies towards the Ottoman Empire.",
      "date": "1919"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gandhi's Support for the Khilafat Movement",
      "text": "Mahatma Gandhi supported the Khilafat Movement in 1920, aligning it with the Indian National Congress's non-cooperation movement against British rule.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Khilafat Conference in Delhi",
      "text": "In November 1919, a major Khilafat Conference was held in Delhi, where leaders from across India gathered to discuss the movement's strategies and objectives.",
      "date": "1919-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Non-Cooperation Movement",
      "text": "In 1920, the Khilafat Movement merged with the Indian National Congress's Non-Cooperation Movement, which called for Indians to boycott British goods and institutions.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Treaty of Sèvres",
      "text": "The Treaty of Sèvres was signed on August 10, 1920, which dismantled the Ottoman Empire and fueled further protests by the Khilafat Movement.",
      "date": "1920-08-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chauri Chaura Incident",
      "text": "On February 4, 1922, the Chauri Chaura incident occurred, where a violent clash led to the suspension of the Non-Cooperation Movement by Gandhi, affecting the Khilafat Movement.",
      "date": "1922-02-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Khilafat Movement",
      "text": "The Khilafat Movement effectively ended in 1922 after the abolition of the Ottoman Caliphate by Mustafa Kemal Atatürk and the failure of the Non-Cooperation Movement.",
      "date": "1922"
    }
  ]
}

