      "headline": "Proposal of the Fifteenth Amendment",
      "text": "The Fifteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution was proposed by Congress on February 26, 1869. It aimed to prohibit the federal government and each state from denying a citizen the right to vote based on race, color, or previous condition of servitude.",
      "date": "1869-02-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ratification of the Fifteenth Amendment",
      "text": "The Fifteenth Amendment was ratified on February 3, 1870. It became the third and last of the Reconstruction Amendments, ensuring that the right to vote could not be denied based on race, color, or previous condition of servitude.",
      "date": "1870-02-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Certification of the Fifteenth Amendment",
      "text": "The Fifteenth Amendment was certified by Secretary of State Hamilton Fish on March 30, 1870, officially adding it to the United States Constitution.",
      "date": "1870-03-30"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First African American Votes",
      "text": "Following the ratification of the Fifteenth Amendment, African Americans began to exercise their right to vote. The first significant use of this right was during the 1870 elections.",
      "date": "1870"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Enforcement Acts",
      "text": "In response to resistance against the Fifteenth Amendment, Congress passed the Enforcement Acts in 1870 and 1871. These acts were designed to protect African American voters and enforce the rights granted by the Fifteenth Amendment.",
      "date": "1870"
    }
  ]
}

