      "headline": "Emergence of Clovis Culture",
      "text": "The Clovis culture emerged around 13,050 years Before Present (BP), which corresponds to approximately 11,050 BCE. This culture is characterized by its distinctive stone tools, particularly the Clovis points.",
      "date": "-11050"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Clovis Points",
      "text": "Clovis points, known for their fluted, lanceolate shape, were developed as part of the Clovis culture's toolkit. These projectile points were multifunctional, serving as both hunting and cutting tools.",
      "date": "-11050"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Clovis Culture Spread",
      "text": "The Clovis culture spread across North America, with sites found from the southwestern United States to the eastern seaboard. This widespread distribution indicates a highly mobile lifestyle.",
      "date": "-11050"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Clovis Culture Peak",
      "text": "The Clovis culture reached its peak around 12,750 years BP, approximately 10,750 BCE. During this time, the culture was widespread across North America.",
      "date": "-10750"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of Clovis Type Site",
      "text": "In 1929, the type site for the Clovis culture was discovered at Blackwater Draw locality No. 1 near Clovis, New Mexico. This site contained stone tools found alongside the remains of Columbian mammoths.",
      "date": "1929"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Identification of Clovis Caches",
      "text": "Over 20 Clovis caches have been identified, where artifacts were stored for later retrieval. These caches provide insight into the resource management and planning of the Clovis people.",
      "date": "1929"
    }
  ]
}

