      "text": "In August 1945, the Potsdam Agreement was signed by the Allies, solidifying the program of denazification as part of the post-war reconstruction of Germany and Austria.",
      "date": "1945-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nuremberg Trials Begin",
      "text": "The Nuremberg Trials, a series of military tribunals held to prosecute prominent leaders of Nazi Germany for war crimes, began on November 20, 1945.",
      "date": "1945-11-20"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Implementation of Denazification Policies",
      "text": "Following the Potsdam Agreement, denazification policies were implemented across Germany and Austria, focusing on removing Nazi Party members from positions of power and influence.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Nuremberg Trials",
      "text": "The Nuremberg Trials concluded on October 1, 1946, with the sentencing of major Nazi war criminals.",
      "date": "1946-10-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Shift in Denazification Policies",
      "text": "By the late 1940s, denazification policies began to shift due to the onset of the Cold War, with a focus on rebuilding Germany as a bulwark against communism.",
      "date": "1947"
