      "headline": "Parliament Declares Yeltsin's Actions Unconstitutional",
      "text": "On September 22, 1993, the Russian parliament declared Yeltsin's decree unconstitutional and voted to impeach him, appointing Vice President Alexander Rutskoy as acting president.",
      "date": "1993-09-22"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Standoff Between Yeltsin and Parliament",
      "text": "Throughout late September 1993, a standoff ensued between Yeltsin and the parliament, with both sides refusing to back down. The parliament barricaded itself in the White House, the Russian parliament building.",
      "date": "1993-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Violence Erupts in Moscow",
      "text": "On October 3, 1993, violence erupted in Moscow as supporters of the parliament clashed with police and attempted to storm the Ostankino television center.",
      "date": "1993-10-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Yeltsin Orders Military Assault",
      "text": "On October 4, 1993, President Yeltsin ordered a military assault on the White House, using tanks and troops to suppress the parliamentary resistance. The building was shelled, and the lawmakers were arrested.",
      "date": "1993-10-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Crisis Ends with Yeltsin's Victory",
      "text": "By October 5, 1993, the constitutional crisis effectively ended with Yeltsin's victory. The military assault had quelled the parliamentary opposition, and Yeltsin's government regained control.",
      "date": "1993-10-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "New Constitution Adopted",
      "text": "On December 12, 1993, a new Russian constitution was adopted via a national referendum, significantly increasing the powers of the presidency and marking the formal end of the crisis.",
