      "headline": "Birth of Jan van Eyck",
      "text": "Jan van Eyck was born around the year 1390. The exact date and place of his birth are not known, but it is believed he was born in the region that is now Belgium.",
      "date": "1390"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Documented Work",
      "text": "Jan van Eyck's first documented work is from 1422, when he was employed as a painter and valet de chambre by John of Bavaria, the ruler of Holland.",
      "date": "1422"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Service to Philip the Good",
      "text": "In 1425, Jan van Eyck entered the service of Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy, as a court painter and diplomat. This position provided him with a steady income and allowed him to travel extensively.",
      "date": "1425"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Ghent Altarpiece",
      "text": "Jan van Eyck completed the Ghent Altarpiece, also known as the Adoration of the Mystic Lamb, in 1432. This monumental polyptych is considered one of his masterpieces and a key work of the Northern Renaissance.",
      "date": "1432"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Marriage",
      "text": "Jan van Eyck married Margaret, about whom little is known, in 1433. She is depicted in some of his paintings.",
      "date": "1433"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Arnolfini Portrait",
      "text": "In 1434, Jan van Eyck painted the Arnolfini Portrait, one of his most famous works, which is celebrated for its intricate detail and use of oil paint.",
      "date": "1434"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Jan van Eyck",
      "text": "Jan van Eyck died on July 9, 1441, in Bruges, which is now part of Belgium. He was buried in the Church of St. Donatian in Bruges.",
      "date": "1441-07-09"
    }
  ]
}

