      "headline": "First Known Use of Gay Panic Defense",
      "text": "The concept of the gay panic defense first emerged in legal cases in the mid-20th century, although it is difficult to pinpoint an exact date for its first use.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Notable Case: Panic Defense in the Murder of Scott Amedure",
      "text": "In 1995, the gay panic defense was used in the trial of Jonathan Schmitz, who was charged with the murder of Scott Amedure. Schmitz claimed he was provoked into a violent rage after Amedure revealed a crush on him during a taping of The Jenny Jones Show.",
      "date": "1995"
    },
    {
      "headline": "California Bans Gay Panic Defense",
      "text": "In 2014, California became the first state in the United States to ban the use of the gay panic defense in court, through the passage of Assembly Bill No. 2501.",
      "date": "2014"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Illinois Bans Gay Panic Defense",
      "text": "In 2017, Illinois followed California's lead and banned the gay panic defense with the signing of Senate Bill 1761.",
      "date": "2017"
    },
    {
      "headline": "New York Bans Gay Panic Defense",
      "text": "In 2019, New York State enacted legislation to prohibit the use of the gay panic defense, joining a growing number of states taking similar actions.",
      "date": "2019"
    },
    {
      "headline": "American Bar Association Urges Ban",
      "text": "The American Bar Association passed a resolution in 2013 urging governments to curtail the availability and effectiveness of the gay panic defense.",
      "date": "2013"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Federal Legislation Introduced",
      "text": "In 2018, the Gay and Trans Panic Defense Prohibition Act was introduced in the United States Congress to ban the defense nationwide, although it has not yet been passed.",
      "date": "2018"
    }
  ]
}

