      "headline": "Discovery of Digoxin",
      "text": "Digoxin is derived from the foxglove plant, Digitalis lanata. The use of foxglove extracts in treating heart conditions dates back to the late 18th century when William Withering published his work on the use of Digitalis in 1785.",
      "date": "1785"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Digoxin in Modern Medicine",
      "text": "Digoxin was introduced into modern medicine in the 20th century as a purified form of the active compounds found in Digitalis. It became one of the oldest medications used in cardiology.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval of Digoxin",
      "text": "Digoxin was approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for medical use. It is used to treat various heart conditions, including atrial fibrillation, atrial flutter, and heart failure.",
      "date": "1954"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Current Use of Digoxin",
      "text": "As of the 21st century, Digoxin continues to be used in the treatment of certain heart conditions, although its use has declined with the development of newer medications.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

