      "headline": "Commissioning of Park Güell",
      "text": "In 1900, Eusebi Güell commissioned Antoni Gaudí to design a residential park intended for the wealthy of Barcelona. The project was inspired by the English garden city movement.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction Begins",
      "text": "Construction of Park Güell began in 1900, with Gaudí overseeing the development of the infrastructure, including roads, viaducts, and the main entrance.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the Dragon Stairway",
      "text": "By 1903, the Dragon Stairway, one of the most iconic features of Park Güell, was completed. It leads to the Hypostyle Room and is adorned with a mosaic-covered dragon sculpture.",
      "date": "1903"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the Hypostyle Room",
      "text": "In 1907, the Hypostyle Room, also known as the Hall of a Hundred Columns, was completed. It features 86 columns and was intended to be a market space for the residential community.",
      "date": "1907"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the Greek Theatre or Nature Square",
      "text": "By 1914, the Greek Theatre, also known as Nature Square, was completed. It was designed as a central gathering space for residents and is surrounded by a serpentine bench covered in colorful mosaics.",
      "date": "1914"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Project Abandonment",
      "text": "In 1914, the residential project was abandoned due to lack of interest from buyers. Only two houses were completed, and the park was left unfinished.",
      "date": "1914"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Opening as a Public Park",
      "text": "In 1926, Park Güell was officially opened to the public as a municipal park, following the death of Antoni Gaudí.",
      "date": "1926"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Designation as a UNESCO World Heritage Site",
      "text": "In 1984, Park Güell was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site as part of the 'Works of Antoni Gaudí' for its cultural significance and unique architectural style.",
      "date": "1984"
    }
  ]
}

