      "headline": "Development of the First Rabies Vaccine",
      "text": "In 1885, the first rabies vaccine was developed by Louis Pasteur and Émile Roux. This vaccine was created using a sample of the virus harvested from infected rabbits, which was then weakened by allowing it to dry for five to ten days.",
      "date": "1885"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Successful Rabies Vaccination",
      "text": "On July 6, 1885, Louis Pasteur administered the first successful rabies vaccination to a nine-year-old boy named Joseph Meister, who had been bitten by a rabid dog. The vaccination was successful, and the boy did not develop rabies.",
      "date": "1885-07-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Human Diploid Cell Vaccine (HDCV)",
      "text": "In 1967, the human diploid cell rabies vaccine (HDCV) was introduced. This vaccine was developed using human diploid cells and became the standard for rabies vaccination due to its safety and effectiveness.",
      "date": "1967"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Approval of Purified Chick Embryo Cell Vaccine (PCECV)",
      "text": "In 1984, the purified chick embryo cell vaccine (PCECV) was approved for use. This vaccine is another option for rabies prevention and is known for its high safety profile.",
      "date": "1984"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Rabies Vaccine for Wildlife",
      "text": "In the 1990s, oral rabies vaccines were developed for wildlife, particularly for controlling rabies in wild animal populations such as raccoons, foxes, and coyotes. This was a significant step in rabies prevention and control.",
      "date": "1990"
    }
  ]
}

