      "headline": "Publication of 'What Is to Be Done?'",
      "text": "In 1902, Vladimir Lenin published 'What Is to Be Done?', a political pamphlet that outlined his vision for a revolutionary vanguard party. This work laid the foundation for Leninism by emphasizing the need for a disciplined party to lead the proletariat in overthrowing capitalism.",
      "date": "1902"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Russian Revolution of 1917",
      "text": "In 1917, Lenin's Bolshevik Party led the October Revolution, which resulted in the overthrow of the Provisional Government and the establishment of a socialist state. This event marked the practical application of Leninist principles in governance.",
      "date": "1917"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Soviet Union",
      "text": "On December 30, 1922, the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (USSR) was officially formed. Leninism became the guiding ideology of the new state, influencing its political and economic structures.",
      "date": "1922-12-30"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lenin's Death",
      "text": "Vladimir Lenin died on January 21, 1924. His death marked the end of his direct influence on the Soviet state, but Leninism continued to shape Soviet policies and communist movements worldwide.",
      "date": "1924-01-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Stalin's Interpretation of Leninism",
      "text": "After Lenin's death, Joseph Stalin rose to power and began to interpret and implement Leninism in a way that emphasized centralized control and rapid industrialization, leading to significant changes in Soviet policy and governance.",
      "date": "1924"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Comintern and the Spread of Leninism",
      "text": "Throughout the 1920s and 1930s, the Communist International (Comintern) worked to spread Leninist ideology globally, supporting communist parties and revolutionary movements in various countries.",
      "date": "1920s-1930s"
    }
  ]
}

