{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Carl Menger Publishes 'Principles of Economics'",
      "text": "In 1871, Carl Menger published 'Principles of Economics' (Grundsätze der Volkswirtschaftslehre), which is considered the founding work of the Austrian School of Economics. Menger's work introduced the theory of marginal utility and laid the groundwork for the Austrian approach to economics.",
      "date": "1871"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eugen von Böhm-Bawerk and Friedrich von Wieser Expand Austrian Economics",
      "text": "In the late 19th century, Eugen von Böhm-Bawerk and Friedrich von Wieser, both students of Carl Menger, expanded on Menger's ideas. Böhm-Bawerk developed the theory of capital and interest, while Wieser contributed to the theory of opportunity cost.",
      "date": "1880-1890"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ludwig von Mises Publishes 'The Theory of Money and Credit'",
      "text": "In 1912, Ludwig von Mises published 'The Theory of Money and Credit', which integrated monetary theory with the Austrian School's principles. This work laid the foundation for the Austrian Business Cycle Theory.",
      "date": "1912"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Friedrich Hayek Joins the Austrian School",
      "text": "In the 1920s, Friedrich Hayek, a prominent economist, became associated with the Austrian School. He later won the Nobel Prize in Economics in 1974 for his work on the theory of money and economic fluctuations.",
      "date": "1920s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ludwig von Mises Publishes 'Human Action'",
      "text": "In 1949, Ludwig von Mises published 'Human Action', a comprehensive treatise on economics that became a seminal work for the Austrian School. It emphasized praxeology, the study of human action, as the basis for economic theory.",
      "date": "1949"
