      "headline": "Introduction of Cadmium Telluride Solar Cells",
      "text": "In the 1970s, research into thin-film solar cells began, including cadmium telluride (CdTe) cells, which would later become a significant part of the solar cell market.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Multi-Junction Solar Cells",
      "text": "In the 1980s, multi-junction solar cells were developed, which use multiple layers of different semiconductor materials to capture a broader range of the solar spectrum and increase efficiency.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Silicon Solar Cells Dominate the Market",
      "text": "By the 1990s, crystalline silicon solar cells became the dominant technology in the photovoltaic market, accounting for the majority of solar cell production.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Advancements in Solar Cell Efficiency",
      "text": "In the 2000s, significant advancements were made in improving the efficiency of solar cells, with some laboratory cells achieving efficiencies over 40%.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Global Solar Capacity Surpasses 100 GW",
      "text": "In 2012, the global installed capacity of solar power surpassed 100 gigawatts, highlighting the rapid growth and adoption of solar technology worldwide.",
      "date": "2012"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Solar Power Becomes the Cheapest Energy Source",
      "text": "By 2020, solar power became the cheapest source of electricity in many parts of the world, driven by technological advancements and economies of scale.",
      "date": "2020"
    }
  ]
}

