      "headline": "Early Interest in Gothic Architecture",
      "text": "In the second half of the 17th century, there was a gradual build-up of interest in medieval Gothic architecture, laying the groundwork for the Gothic Revival movement.",
      "date": "1650"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Horace Walpole's Strawberry Hill",
      "text": "In 1749, Horace Walpole began transforming his home, Strawberry Hill, into a Gothic Revival masterpiece, marking one of the earliest examples of the movement.",
      "date": "1749"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'The Castle of Otranto'",
      "text": "In 1764, Horace Walpole published 'The Castle of Otranto', a novel that helped popularize Gothic themes and aesthetics, influencing the Gothic Revival movement.",
      "date": "1764"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Pugin's 'Contrasts'",
      "text": "In 1836, Augustus Pugin published 'Contrasts', a book that argued for the revival of Gothic architecture as a moral and aesthetic ideal.",
      "date": "1836"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the Houses of Parliament",
      "text": "In 1870, the new Houses of Parliament in London, designed by Charles Barry and Augustus Pugin, were completed, showcasing the height of Gothic Revival architecture.",
      "date": "1870"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of Gothic Revival",
      "text": "By the 1880s and early 1890s, Gothic Revival began to fall out of fashion as architectural tastes shifted towards other styles.",
      "date": "1885"
    }
  ]
}

