      "headline": "Introduction of Group B Regulations",
      "text": "In 1982, the Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile (FIA) introduced Group B regulations for grand touring (GT) vehicles used in sports car racing and rallying. These regulations allowed for high-performance vehicles with fewer restrictions on technology and design.",
      "date": "1982"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Group B in World Rally Championship",
      "text": "Group B cars became the highest class used in the World Rally Championship from 1982 to 1986. They were known for their powerful engines and lightweight construction, which made them extremely fast and popular in the rallying scene.",
      "date": "1982"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak Popularity and Performance",
      "text": "During the mid-1980s, Group B cars reached peak popularity and performance. Manufacturers like Audi, Peugeot, Lancia, and Ford developed iconic models such as the Audi Quattro, Peugeot 205 T16, Lancia Delta S4, and Ford RS200.",
      "date": "1984"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Safety Concerns and Fatal Accidents",
      "text": "By 1986, safety concerns arose due to the high speeds and power of Group B cars, leading to several fatal accidents involving drivers and spectators. This raised questions about the safety of the cars and the regulations governing them.",
      "date": "1986"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ban of Group B Cars",
      "text": "In 1986, following a series of fatal accidents, the FIA decided to ban Group B cars from rallying. The 1986 season was the last time Group B cars competed in the World Rally Championship, marking the end of an era in rallying.",
      "date": "1986"
    }
  ]
}

