      "headline": "Beginning of the Highland Clearances",
      "text": "The Highland Clearances began in the mid-18th century, around 1750, as landlords sought to increase their income by replacing people with sheep, which were more profitable.",
      "date": "1750"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Phase of Clearances",
      "text": "The first phase of the Highland Clearances occurred from 1780 to 1820, characterized by the forced eviction of tenants to make way for sheep farming.",
      "date": "1780"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kildonan Clearances",
      "text": "In 1813, the Kildonan Clearances took place in Sutherland, where many families were evicted to make way for sheep farming.",
      "date": "1813"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Phase of Clearances",
      "text": "The second phase of the Highland Clearances took place from 1820 to 1854, marked by more aggressive and widespread evictions.",
      "date": "1820"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sutherland Clearances",
      "text": "Between 1814 and 1820, the Sutherland Clearances were among the most notorious, involving the eviction of thousands of people.",
      "date": "1814"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Glencalvie Clearances",
      "text": "In 1845, the Glencalvie Clearances occurred, where tenants were evicted and their homes destroyed.",
      "date": "1845"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Crofters' Holdings (Scotland) Act 1886",
      "text": "In 1886, the Crofters' Holdings (Scotland) Act was passed, providing some security of tenure for crofters and marking the end of the most severe phase of the Highland Clearances.",
      "date": "1886"
    }
  ]
}

