{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "The Jewish Question Emerges",
      "text": "The term 'Jewish question' emerged in the 19th century as part of a broader debate in Europe concerning the status and treatment of Jews. This debate was particularly prominent in the context of Jewish emancipation and the integration of Jews into European society.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Jewish Emancipation in France",
      "text": "In 1791, France became the first European country to emancipate its Jewish population, granting them equal rights as citizens. This set a precedent for other European countries and was a significant milestone in the Jewish question.",
      "date": "1791"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Hep-Hep Riots",
      "text": "In 1819, a series of anti-Jewish riots known as the Hep-Hep riots broke out in Germany, highlighting the tensions surrounding the Jewish question and the resistance to Jewish emancipation.",
      "date": "1819"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Damascus Affair",
      "text": "In 1840, the Damascus affair, a case of blood libel against Jews in Syria, drew international attention and highlighted the persistent prejudices and challenges faced by Jewish communities.",
      "date": "1840"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Dreyfus Affair",
      "text": "The Dreyfus affair, beginning in 1894, was a political scandal in France involving the wrongful conviction of Jewish army officer Alfred Dreyfus for treason. It exposed deep-seated anti-Semitism and was a pivotal moment in the Jewish question.",
      "date": "1894"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Theodor Herzl and Zionism",
      "text": "In 1896, Theodor Herzl published 'Der Judenstaat' (The Jewish State), advocating for the establishment of a Jewish state as a solution to the Jewish question. This marked the beginning of political Zionism.",
      "date": "1896"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Balfour Declaration",
      "text": "In 1917, the British government issued the Balfour Declaration, expressing support for the establishment of a 'national home for the Jewish people' in Palestine, influencing the Jewish question in the context of national aspirations.",
      "date": "1917"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Holocaust",
      "text": "During World War II, the Holocaust resulted in the systematic genocide of six million Jews by Nazi Germany, representing the most extreme and tragic outcome of the unresolved Jewish question in Europe.",
      "date": "1941"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the State of Israel",
      "text": "In 1948, the State of Israel was established, providing a national homeland for Jews and significantly altering the dynamics of the Jewish question by providing a sovereign state for Jewish people.",
      "date": "1948"
    }
  ]
}

