      "headline": "Construction of South Fork Dam",
      "text": "The South Fork Dam was originally constructed between 1838 and 1853 as part of a canal system to be used for transportation.",
      "date": "1838"
    },
    {
      "headline": "South Fork Dam Purchased by South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club",
      "text": "In 1879, the South Fork Dam was purchased by the South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club, a group of wealthy Pittsburgh industrialists, who made modifications to the dam.",
      "date": "1879"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Heavy Rainfall Begins",
      "text": "In late May 1889, several days of extremely heavy rainfall began, leading to rising water levels in the reservoir behind the South Fork Dam.",
      "date": "1889-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "South Fork Dam Failure",
      "text": "On May 31, 1889, the South Fork Dam catastrophically failed, releasing 14.55 million cubic meters of water into the valley below.",
      "date": "1889-05-31"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Johnstown Flood",
      "text": "The floodwaters reached Johnstown, Pennsylvania, causing massive destruction. The flood killed 2,208 people and caused approximately $17,000,000 in damage.",
      "date": "1889-05-31"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Aftermath and Relief Efforts",
      "text": "In the days following the flood, relief efforts were organized to aid survivors and rebuild the town. Clara Barton and the American Red Cross were among those who provided assistance.",
      "date": "1889-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Investigation and Legal Proceedings",
      "text": "In the aftermath of the flood, investigations were conducted to determine the cause of the dam failure. Legal proceedings were initiated, but no one was held legally responsible.",
      "date": "1889"
    }
  ]
}

