      "headline": "Aristotle's Treatise on Colors",
      "text": "Aristotle's treatise 'On Colors' is one of the earliest known works discussing the behavior of colors. Although the exact date is uncertain, it is believed to have been written in the 4th century BCE.",
      "date": "-0350"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Isaac Newton's Theory of Color",
      "text": "In 1672, Isaac Newton published his theory of color, which was based on the observation that a prism could decompose white light into the colors of the visible spectrum.",
      "date": "1672"
    },
    {
      "headline": "18th Century Formalization of Color Theory",
      "text": "The formalization of color theory began in the 18th century, initially within a controversy over Isaac Newton's theory of color and the nature of primary colors.",
      "date": "1700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Johann Wolfgang von Goethe's 'Theory of Colours'",
      "text": "In 1810, Johann Wolfgang von Goethe published 'Theory of Colours', which challenged Newton's ideas and emphasized the subjective experience of color.",
      "date": "1810"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chevreul's Law of Simultaneous Contrast",
      "text": "In 1839, Michel Eugène Chevreul published his 'Law of Simultaneous Contrast of Colors', which described how colors can affect each other when placed side by side.",
      "date": "1839"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Color Wheel",
      "text": "Throughout the 19th century, the color wheel was developed as a tool for artists to understand color relationships and create harmonious color schemes.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Schism between Traditional Color Theory and Color Science",
      "text": "By the end of the 19th century, a schism had formed between traditional color theory and color science, with the former focusing on artistic applications and the latter on scientific and functional applications.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

