      "headline": "Holy Roman Empire Eagle",
      "text": "The eagle was used as a symbol of the Holy Roman Empire, which existed from 962 to 1806. The imperial eagle was a black eagle with a red beak and claws on a gold field.",
      "date": "0962"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reichsadler in the German Confederation",
      "text": "After the dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire in 1806, the German Confederation (1815–1866) adopted a similar eagle as its symbol.",
      "date": "1815"
    },
    {
      "headline": "North German Confederation and German Empire",
      "text": "The North German Confederation (1867–1871) and subsequently the German Empire (1871–1918) used a version of the eagle known as the Reichsadler.",
      "date": "1867"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Weimar Republic Adoption",
      "text": "The Weimar Republic (1919–1933) adopted a simplified version of the Reichsadler as its coat of arms, which was a black eagle with a red beak and claws on a gold field.",
      "date": "1919"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nazi Germany's Reichsadler",
      "text": "During the Nazi regime (1933–1945), the Reichsadler was modified to include a swastika, which was placed within a wreath held by the eagle's talons.",
      "date": "1933"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Federal Republic of Germany (West Germany) Adoption",
      "text": "After World War II, the Federal Republic of Germany (West Germany) was established in 1949 and adopted the Bundesadler, a version of the eagle similar to that of the Weimar Republic.",
      "date": "1949"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reunification of Germany",
      "text": "Following the reunification of Germany in 1990, the Bundesadler continued to be used as the coat of arms of the unified Germany.",
      "date": "1990"
    }
  ]
}

