    {
      "headline": "Discovery of Histamine",
      "text": "Histamine was discovered in 1910. It is an organic nitrogenous compound involved in local immune responses, communication, and regulating physiological functions in the gut, as well as acting as a neurotransmitter.",
      "date": "1910"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition as a Local Hormone",
      "text": "Histamine has been considered a local hormone (autocoid) because it is produced without the involvement of the classic endocrine glands.",
      "date": "1910"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Role in Immune Response",
      "text": "Histamine is involved in the inflammatory response and has a central role as a mediator of itching. It is produced by basophils and mast cells as part of an immune response to foreign pathogens.",
      "date": "1910"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition as a Central Neurotransmitter",
      "text": "In recent years, histamine has been recognized as a central neurotransmitter, indicating its broader role in the brain, spinal cord, and uterus.",
      "date": "Recent years"
    }
  ]
}

