      "text": "Hun Sen was born on August 5, 1952, in Kampong Cham Province, Cambodia.",
      "date": "1952-08-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Joins Khmer Rouge",
      "text": "In 1970, Hun Sen joined the Khmer Rouge, a communist movement that would later take control of Cambodia.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Defects to Vietnam",
      "text": "In 1977, Hun Sen defected to Vietnam as the Khmer Rouge regime began purges against its own members.",
      "date": "1977"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Becomes Foreign Minister",
      "text": "In 1979, after the Vietnamese invasion of Cambodia, Hun Sen was appointed as the Foreign Minister of the People's Republic of Kampuchea.",
      "date": "1979"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Appointed Prime Minister",
      "text": "Hun Sen was appointed as the Prime Minister of Cambodia on January 14, 1985, becoming one of the youngest heads of government in the world at that time.",
      "date": "1985-01-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Paris Peace Agreements",
      "text": "In 1991, Hun Sen was a signatory to the Paris Peace Agreements, which aimed to end the Cambodian-Vietnamese War and establish peace in Cambodia.",
      "date": "1991"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Co-Prime Minister",
      "text": "Following the 1993 elections, Hun Sen became the Second Prime Minister of Cambodia, sharing power with Prince Norodom Ranariddh.",
      "date": "1993"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Coup and Sole Prime Minister",
      "text": "In 1997, Hun Sen staged a coup against his co-Prime Minister, Norodom Ranariddh, and became the sole Prime Minister of Cambodia.",
      "date": "1997"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Re-elected Prime Minister",
      "text": "Hun Sen was re-elected as Prime Minister in 1998, solidifying his control over Cambodian politics.",
      "date": "1998"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Longest-Serving Prime Minister",
      "text": "By 2018, Hun Sen became the longest-serving Prime Minister in Cambodian history, having held the position for over 30 years.",
      "date": "2018"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Steps Down as Prime Minister",
      "text": "In August 2023, Hun Sen stepped down as Prime Minister, passing the position to his son, Hun Manet.",
      "date": "2023-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Becomes President of the Senate",
      "text": "In 2024, Hun Sen assumed the role of President of the Senate in Cambodia.",
      "date": "2024"
    }
  ]
}

