      "text": "The earliest known evidence of the Mesoamerican ballgame dates back to around 1650 BCE. Archaeological findings suggest that the game was played by the pre-Columbian people of Ancient Mesoamerica.",
      "date": "-1650"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Olmec Civilization and the Ballgame",
      "text": "The Olmec civilization, which flourished from around 1500 BCE to 400 BCE, is believed to have been one of the first cultures to play the Mesoamerican ballgame. The Olmecs are known for creating large stone heads and are considered one of the earliest complex societies in Mesoamerica.",
      "date": "-1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ballgame in the Classic Maya Period",
      "text": "During the Classic Maya period (circa 250 CE to 900 CE), the ballgame was an important cultural and ritual activity. It was often associated with the Maya creation myth and was depicted in various artworks and inscriptions.",
      "date": "0250"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ballgame in the Postclassic Period",
      "text": "In the Postclassic period (circa 900 CE to 1521 CE), the ballgame continued to be played by various Mesoamerican cultures, including the Aztecs. The game was often associated with religious and political significance.",
      "date": "0900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spanish Conquest and Decline of the Ballgame",
      "text": "With the Spanish conquest of Mesoamerica in the early 16th century, the traditional Mesoamerican ballgame began to decline. The Spanish viewed the game as pagan and suppressed its practice.",
      "date": "1521"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Revival: Ulama",
      "text": "A modernized version of the Mesoamerican ballgame, known as ulama, is still played today by indigenous populations in some regions of Mexico. This version of the game preserves many elements of the ancient sport.",
      "date": "2023"
    }
  ]
}

