      "text": "The origins of hanbok can be traced back to the Three Kingdoms of Korea (57 BCE – 668 CE), where the basic structure of the hanbok was established.",
      "date": "-0057"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Goryeo Dynasty",
      "text": "During the Goryeo Dynasty (918–1392), hanbok styles began to evolve with influences from the Mongol Yuan Dynasty, leading to changes in design and materials.",
      "date": "0918"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Joseon Dynasty",
      "text": "The Joseon Dynasty (1392–1897) saw the hanbok develop into its modern form, with distinct styles for different social classes and occasions.",
      "date": "1392"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Late Joseon Period",
      "text": "In the late Joseon period, the hanbok became more elaborate, with vibrant colors and intricate designs, reflecting the wearer's social status.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Japanese Occupation",
      "text": "During the Japanese occupation of Korea (1910–1945), the wearing of hanbok was discouraged, and Western clothing became more prevalent.",
      "date": "1910"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-Korean War",
      "text": "After the Korean War (1950–1953), there was a revival of interest in traditional Korean culture, including the hanbok, which became a symbol of national identity.",
      "date": "1953"
    },
