      "headline": "Establishment of Verkhovna Rada",
      "text": "The Verkhovna Rada was established as the legislative body of the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic, which was part of the Soviet Union, in 1938.",
      "date": "1938"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Declaration of Independence",
      "text": "On August 24, 1991, the Verkhovna Rada declared Ukraine's independence from the Soviet Union, marking a significant turning point in the country's history.",
      "date": "1991-08-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption of the Constitution of Ukraine",
      "text": "The Verkhovna Rada adopted the Constitution of Ukraine on June 28, 1996, establishing the framework for the country's government and legal system.",
      "date": "1996-06-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Orange Revolution",
      "text": "In late 2004, the Verkhovna Rada was at the center of the political crisis known as the Orange Revolution, which led to a re-run of the presidential election due to widespread allegations of electoral fraud.",
      "date": "2004"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Euromaidan Protests",
      "text": "Starting in November 2013, the Verkhovna Rada was involved in the political upheaval known as the Euromaidan protests, which eventually led to the ousting of President Viktor Yanukovych in February 2014.",
      "date": "2013-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decentralization Reform",
      "text": "In 2015, the Verkhovna Rada passed significant decentralization reforms aimed at transferring more powers to local governments in Ukraine.",
      "date": "2015"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Martial Law Declaration",
      "text": "On November 26, 2018, the Verkhovna Rada approved the imposition of martial law in parts of Ukraine following a naval confrontation with Russia in the Kerch Strait.",
      "date": "2018-11-26"
    },
