      "text": "In 1918, the FTC brought its first major antitrust case against the American Tobacco Company, marking the beginning of its role in enforcing antitrust laws.",
      "date": "1918"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Wheeler-Lea Act",
      "text": "The Wheeler-Lea Act was passed in 1938, amending the Federal Trade Commission Act to include the prohibition of unfair or deceptive acts or practices in commerce, expanding the FTC's role in consumer protection.",
      "date": "1938"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Magnuson-Moss Warranty Act",
      "text": "In 1975, the Magnuson-Moss Warranty Act was enacted, giving the FTC the authority to regulate consumer product warranties and improve consumer protection.",
      "date": "1975"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FTC's Role in the Digital Age",
      "text": "In the late 1990s and early 2000s, the FTC began to address issues related to the internet and digital commerce, including privacy, data security, and online advertising.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Do Not Call Implementation Act",
      "text": "In 2003, the FTC implemented the National Do Not Call Registry, allowing consumers to opt-out of telemarketing calls, significantly impacting consumer privacy and telemarketing practices.",
      "date": "2003"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FTC's Focus on Tech Companies",
      "text": "In the 2010s, the FTC increased its focus on large technology companies, investigating issues related to antitrust, data privacy, and consumer protection.",
      "date": "2010"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
