      "headline": "First Description by Moritz Kaposi",
      "text": "Kaposi's sarcoma was first described by the Hungarian dermatologist Moritz Kaposi in 1872. He identified it as a rare form of skin cancer that primarily affected elderly men of Mediterranean or Eastern European Jewish descent.",
      "date": "1872"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Identification of Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)",
      "text": "In 1994, Human Herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8) was identified as the causative agent of Kaposi's sarcoma. This discovery was crucial in understanding the pathogenesis of the disease.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Association with HIV/AIDS",
      "text": "In the early 1980s, Kaposi's sarcoma became widely known as one of the opportunistic infections associated with HIV/AIDS. It was one of the first diseases to be linked to the emerging AIDS epidemic.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Antiretroviral Therapy",
      "text": "The development and widespread use of antiretroviral therapy (ART) in the mid-1990s significantly reduced the incidence of Kaposi's sarcoma in HIV-positive individuals by improving immune function.",
      "date": "1996"
    }
  ]
}

