      "headline": "Emergence of Neoclassical Architecture",
      "text": "The Neoclassical movement began in the mid-18th century in Italy, France, and Germany. It sought to return to a purer form of classical architecture, moving away from the excesses of the Late Baroque style.",
      "date": "1750"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence of Classical Antiquity",
      "text": "Neoclassical architecture was heavily influenced by the architecture of ancient Rome and Greece. This was a reaction against the ornate styles of the Renaissance and Baroque periods.",
      "date": "1750"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spread Across Europe",
      "text": "By the late 18th century, Neoclassical architecture had spread across Europe, becoming one of the most prominent architectural styles in the Western world.",
      "date": "1780"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption in the United States",
      "text": "Neoclassical architecture was adopted in the United States, where it became associated with the ideals of democracy and civic virtue, influencing the design of many government buildings.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak Popularity",
      "text": "Neoclassical architecture reached its peak popularity in the early 19th century, with notable examples including the British Museum in London and the White House in Washington, D.C.",
      "date": "1820"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline and Legacy",
      "text": "By the mid-19th century, Neoclassical architecture began to decline as new styles such as Gothic Revival and later Modernism emerged. However, its influence persisted in various forms, particularly in civic and institutional architecture.",
      "date": "1850"
    }
  ]
}

