      "text": "The Twenty-sixth Amendment to the United States Constitution was proposed by Congress on March 23, 1971. This amendment aimed to establish a nationally standardized minimum voting age of 18 for participation in state and federal elections.",
      "date": "1971-03-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ratification by the States",
      "text": "By July 1, 1971, three-fourths of the states had ratified the Twenty-sixth Amendment, making it part of the United States Constitution. This rapid ratification process was one of the fastest in U.S. history.",
      "date": "1971-07-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Certification of the Amendment",
      "text": "On July 5, 1971, the Twenty-sixth Amendment was certified by the Administrator of General Services, officially lowering the voting age to 18 across the United States.",
      "date": "1971-07-05"
    }
  ]
}

