      "text": "The final Constitution of South Africa was adopted on December 4, 1996, which further defined the structure and powers of the National Assembly.",
      "date": "1996-12-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Session of the National Assembly",
      "text": "The first session of the National Assembly under the new Constitution was held in 1997, marking the beginning of its role as the legislative body of South Africa.",
      "date": "1997"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Proportional Representation System",
      "text": "The National Assembly uses a party-list proportional representation system, where half of the members are elected from nine provincial lists and the other half from national lists. This system was introduced to ensure proportionality in representation.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "2009 General Elections",
      "text": "The general elections held in 2009 resulted in the African National Congress (ANC) winning a majority of seats in the National Assembly.",
      "date": "2009"
    },
    {
      "headline": "2014 General Elections",
      "text": "The 2014 general elections saw the ANC retaining its majority in the National Assembly, although with a reduced margin compared to previous elections.",
      "date": "2014"
    },
    {
      "headline": "2019 General Elections",
      "text": "In the 2019 general elections, the ANC once again won the majority of seats in the National Assembly, continuing its dominance in South African politics.",
      "date": "2019"
    }
  ]
}

