      "text": "In January 1889, Wovoka, a Northern Paiute spiritual leader, experienced a vision during a solar eclipse. He claimed that this vision revealed the Ghost Dance, a new religious movement that promised the resurrection of the Native American ancestors, the removal of the white settlers, and the restoration of the Native American way of life.",
      "date": "1889-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spread of the Ghost Dance",
      "text": "By the late 1880s, the Ghost Dance movement had spread rapidly among various Native American tribes, including the Lakota Sioux, who adopted the dance as a symbol of hope and resistance against U.S. government policies.",
      "date": "1889"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Ghost Dance and the Lakota Sioux",
      "text": "In 1890, the Ghost Dance movement gained significant traction among the Lakota Sioux, who were facing severe oppression and hardship due to U.S. government policies. The dance was seen as a way to restore their culture and autonomy.",
      "date": "1890"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Wounded Knee Massacre",
      "text": "On December 29, 1890, the U.S. Army's 7th Cavalry Regiment surrounded a group of Lakota Ghost Dancers near Wounded Knee Creek in South Dakota. A confrontation led to the massacre of approximately 150-300 Lakota men, women, and children, marking a tragic end to the Ghost Dance movement.",
      "date": "1890-12-29"
    }
  ]
}

