      "headline": "Discovery of Streptococcus pneumoniae",
      "text": "Streptococcus pneumoniae was first discovered in 1881 by the U.S. Army physician George Sternberg and the French chemist Louis Pasteur, who independently isolated the organism from saliva.",
      "date": "1881"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Identification as a Major Cause of Pneumonia",
      "text": "In the late 19th century, Streptococcus pneumoniae was recognized as a major cause of pneumonia, leading to its name pneumococcus.",
      "date": "1890"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Pneumococcal Vaccine",
      "text": "The first pneumococcal vaccine was developed in the 1940s, which targeted multiple strains of the bacterium.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Polysaccharide Vaccine",
      "text": "In 1977, the polysaccharide vaccine was introduced, which provided protection against 14 serotypes of Streptococcus pneumoniae.",
      "date": "1977"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Conjugate Vaccine",
      "text": "In 2000, the pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV7) was introduced, which covered 7 serotypes and was designed to be more effective in young children.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Expanded Conjugate Vaccine",
      "text": "In 2010, the pneumococcal conjugate vaccine was expanded to include 13 serotypes (PCV13), providing broader protection.",
      "date": "2010"
    }
  ]
}

